menu "BlueAudio Puck: audio output"

    config PUCK_I2S_BCK_GPIO
        int "I2S bit clock (BCK) GPIO"
        default 26
        range 0 39
        help
            Connects to BCK on the PCM5102A breakout.

    config PUCK_I2S_LRCK_GPIO
        int "I2S word select (LRCK) GPIO"
        default 25
        range 0 39
        help
            Connects to LRCK/LCK on the PCM5102A breakout.

    config PUCK_I2S_DOUT_GPIO
        int "I2S data out (DIN) GPIO"
        default 22
        range 0 39
        help
            Connects to DIN on the PCM5102A breakout.

    choice PUCK_I2S_SLOT_FORMAT
        prompt "I2S frame format"
        default PUCK_I2S_FORMAT_PHILIPS
        help
            Must match the DAC's format pin. On a PCM5102A breakout, FMT tied to
            GND selects I2S (Philips); FMT tied high selects left-justified.

        config PUCK_I2S_FORMAT_PHILIPS
            bool "I2S (Philips) -- PCM5102A with FMT low"
        config PUCK_I2S_FORMAT_MSB
            bool "Left-justified (MSB) -- PCM5102A with FMT high"
    endchoice

    config PUCK_I2S_USE_APLL
        bool "Clock I2S from the audio PLL"
        default y
        help
            The audio PLL hits 44.1 kHz far more precisely than the default PLL
            divider does, which avoids slow sample slip on long tracks. It costs
            a small amount of extra current, so turn it off if runtime matters
            more than pitch accuracy.

    config PUCK_AUDIO_RINGBUF_KB
        int "Audio ring buffer size (kB)"
        default 32
        range 8 64
        help
            Larger buffers ride out Wi-Fi coexistence and radio retries at the
            cost of internal RAM and added latency. 32 kB is roughly 185 ms of
            stereo 16-bit 44.1 kHz audio.

    config PUCK_AUDIO_PREFETCH_PERCENT
        int "Prefetch level before playback starts (% of buffer)"
        default 25
        range 10 90
        help
            Playback waits until the buffer is this full, then runs until it is
            empty, so this is also what every underrun costs to rebuild.

            At the 32 kB default, 25% is about 46 ms -- comfortably above the
            33 ms the I2S DMA chain already holds, and above typical BR/EDR
            retry windows. The old 60% was 111 ms, which meant an audible 78 ms
            gap after every underrun and enough added latency to break lip-sync
            on video.

    config PUCK_AUDIO_WRITER_CORE
        int "CPU core for the I2S writer task"
        default 1
        range 0 1
        help
            Keep this off core 0, which runs the Bluetooth controller and stack.

endmenu
